Every program gets its own name in the Dock: its Mac Driver AppName, else its exe name.

macOS labels a Wine program in the Dock after the file it was exec'd as.
The loader's hack 22144 already execs a hard link named after the exe
($TMPDIR/winetemp-<loader id>/<name>), but on a build without the preloader
it only did so when WINEDLLPATH was set, so on this engine every program
showed as "wine", and the first program was named after the wine binary.

Now:
- the link is made when WINEDLLPATH is set or the loader sits beside its
  ntdll.so (lib/wine/x86_64-unix/wine), which then finds ntdll through the
  ntdll.so link create_tempdir already puts next to it;
- on the first start the program on the command line names it, not "wine";
- spawn_process reads HKCU\Software\Wine\AppDefaults\<app.exe>\Mac Driver,
  AppName, in the parent (the forked child must not talk to the server) and
  passes it as WINEMACAPPNAME, or clears it, so a program Steam starts gets
  its own name and never inherits Steam's. Whoever starts the first program
  may set WINEMACAPPNAME. The name also reaches the menu bar through
  WINEPRELOADERAPPNAME, as before. '/' and ':' become spaces.

No AppName and no WINEMACAPPNAME: the exe name, as hack 22144 intended.

Two fixes to hack 22144 itself, found by testing (dock_label_child):
- the named file is an APFS clone, not a hard link. Hard links are one file
  under many names, and macOS reports a running program's path as whichever
  of those names it looked up last: every program showed the same, wrong
  name (a notepad started by HostApp came out as "explorer.exe"). A clone is
  a file of its own, takes no extra space, and keeps its name. Hard link and
  symlink stay as fallbacks.
- extract_exe_name computed the name without its extension but returned the
  whole string, so programs showed "explorer.exe" instead of "explorer".

